A||||4||kslish||CD changer wiring advice||||1||
Z||000000||kslish||07-31-2001||11:46 PM||||I spent an hour or so installing a new CD changer in one of my Fieros tonight.  I've made most of the wiring connections and ran the wiring through the center console from where the changer is mounted (behind the passenger seat) to the head unit.  I also grounded the power supply.<P>To finish the installation, I need ideas on where to make the final two connections.  I need a constant always-on hot and a ignition switched hot (the installation manual suggests running off the radio fuse for this one).  <P>I'd prefer to not make any permanent modifications to the stock wiring.  In one of my other Fieros I have a secondary lead off the battery for an amp so that is where I also connected the power for the changer in that car.  This Fiero has a basically stock system with upgraded speakers and the stock sub.   <P>I don't believe changers require that much power, so I've been thinking using those spade tap things that wrap around the leg of a fuse in the fuse box and then connect the wires there.  <P>Anyone got any advice or tips on the best place to make these connections?<P><P>------------------<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR>See them at <A HREF="http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://homepage.mac.com/kjslish/Fiero.html</A><BR>Western PA Fiero Club - <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||08-01-2001||12:06 AM||||Why don't you tap off the connections for the radio. The stock Fiero radio will have both hot and keyed power connections. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Indiana_resto_guy||08-01-2001||03:23 AM||||Take your wires all the way to your fuse box.<BR>The connections you need are there!||||reg||1
Z||000003||862M4inCA||08-01-2001||09:11 AM||||I installed an aftermarket CD changer in the same location you did, and what I ended up doing what using the cigarette lighter hot wire for my constant connection and found a wire running from the fuse box that was only hot when the key was on, not in ACC. I haven't had any problems with blowing fuses, but I have a feeling that it's due to the fact that mine is just the base coupe with PW and tilt being the ONLY options this car has so the wiring isn't loaded with a bunch of extra accesories also pulling power. Otherwise I agree with Indiana_resto_guy since the fuse panel has several points to get power depending on the position of the ignition switch, and I believe they're also labeled.||||reg||1
Z||000004||varnster||08-01-2001||05:25 PM||||just tie into the keyed hot wire going to your amp, when the the amp is on, the changer is on.just make sure you got an inline fuse just for the changer, and you got good metel for the ground!||||reg||12
